  "published": "2026-09-26T12:55:21.367Z",
  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "26.9.27 stamps the net10.0 assembly with the package version. 26.9.14 shipped it as 1.0.0.0 - the publish workflow's test step rebuilt that slice without the version before packing - while the Android, iOS, Mac Catalyst and Windows assemblies were right. Nothing else changed.\n26.9.9 renames the assemblies and namespaces to match the package ID. 26.9.8 shipped the correct package ID around assemblies still called Mauime.*, so consuming it meant writing using Mauime.X against a package called Me.Toolkit.Maui.X; it is unlisted. Nothing else changed.\nFirst release. Replaces Xamarinme.WebHostPatch 1.0.0.\nThe approach is unchanged, because it is the only one that works: ASP.NET Core as netstandard2.0 packages, copied into the app like any assembly. The Microsoft.AspNetCore.App shared framework ships no runtime pack for android, ios or maccatalyst, so a framework reference compiles a library and then fails the consuming app with NETSDK1082.\nWhat changed is the version and the patching. The packages are the 2.3.x servicing line rather than 2.2.0, which means no known advisories - 2.2.0 carries a critical one in Microsoft.AspNetCore.Server.Kestrel.Core (GHSA-5rrx-jjjq-q2r5) and a moderate one in Microsoft.AspNetCore.Server.IIS (GHSA-prrf-397v-83xh).\nBoth of Xamarinme's forks of Microsoft's code are gone. The vendored Microsoft.Extensions.Primitives, stamped 5.9.0.0 to outrank the real assembly, existed because Microsoft.Net.Http.Headers 2.2.0 called an InplaceStringBuilder that Primitives 5.0 had deleted; 2.3.11 no longer references that type. The WebHostExtensions fork existed because Console.CancelKeyPress threw on Mono; that call lives in RunAsync, and this starts and stops the host explicitly instead, which is what an app wants anyway. The generic host's ConsoleLifetime is not involved either.\nIMeToolkitMauiWebHost starts and stops an IWebHost on Kestrel and reports the address it bound to, read from the server rather than from the options, so a port of 0 reports the port the operating system chose. Listening on every interface reports the machine's LAN address rather than 0.0.0.0.\nNetworkAddress.GetLocalAddress replaces the Xamarinme demo's NetworkHelper, with its bug fixed: that version picked the first qualifying interface and only then filtered its addresses, so a machine whose first interface held only a link-local address reported none at all.\nWorth knowing if you are upgrading: Xamarinme.WebHostPatch 1.0.0 pins ASP.NET Core 2.2.0, which carries a critical advisory in Microsoft.AspNetCore.Server.Kestrel.Core (GHSA-5rrx-jjjq-q2r5) and a moderate one in Microsoft.AspNetCore.Server.IIS (GHSA-prrf-397v-83xh). Its forked Primitives also shadows the real assembly for anything sharing an output directory.",
